   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "**Python result** (`span_full.py`, `pairword_check.py`): every listed word localises and equals $\\lambda\\times$(the 1-pair word) exactly. The localising set found by scanning all products up to length 4 of $t_k(\\theta_F+mh)$ ($k=1,2,3$, $m=-2..5$) has 25 words, and their span is **5-dimensional**: $\\{1,X_1,X_2,X_3,X_3X_1\\}$, with $X_1=t_1(\\theta_F)$, $X_2=t_2(\\theta_F+h)$, $X_3=\\hat t_3(\\theta_F+h)=\\hat t_3(\\theta_A-h)$. The products add nothing new: e.g. $t_1(\\theta_F)t_1(\\theta_F+h)$ acts like $X_2$ on $W_a$, and $t_1(\\theta_F)\\hat t_3(\\theta_F+3h)$ and $t_1(\\theta_A)X_3$ are trivial on weight states.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "**DISCREPANCY (2026-10-13 rerun), cell 17.** The port compared each localised pair state with $(\\prod_i\\lambda_{k_i}(u_i))\\times$(1-pair word). That holds exactly for the empty word, the three single operators and the word $t_1(\\theta_F)$ then $\\hat t_3(\\theta_F+h)$ (the $X_3X_1$ word), but is **False for the other four product words**: $t_1(\\theta_F)t_1(\\theta_F+h)$, $t_1(\\theta_F)\\hat t_3(\\theta_F+3h)$, $t_1(\\theta_F+2h)\\hat t_3(\\theta_F+h)$, $\\hat t_3(\\theta_F+h)\\hat t_3(\\theta_F+2h)$. For those the localised state is still exactly proportional to the 1-pair word (this is what the python `pairword_check.py` tested: it fitted $\\lambda$ as a ratio, it did not use the product formula), with constant / $\\prod\\lambda$ = 51/31, 71/31, 71/31, 51/31. So the statement 'localises and equals const x (1-pair word)' is reproduced for all 9 words; the closed product formula for the constant is reproduced only for the first 5 words. Reason (diagnosis): the second factor of a product acts on a pair-1 state that is not the pair vacuum, so the column-support lemma does not apply to it and the rest sites contribute a different constant. This does not affect $K_\\kappa$ (only the $X_3X_1$ word is a product, and its constant is $\\lambda_1\\lambda_3$, see cell 26). Rank of the localised pair states: 5 (reproduced).\n\n(fixed 2026-10-13: cell 17 now also tests proportionality to the 1-pair word and prints fitted constant / $\\prod\\lambda$; the old column compared to $\\prod\\lambda$ only. Cells 11, 14 each take 3-5 min here, not seconds.)",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### 5b. The five directions on the pair vacuum, generic symbolic twist",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "Module[{g, G, vac, x1, x2, x3},\n G = Array[g, {4, 4}]; vac = pgProd[{pgUV[1], pgUV[4]}];\n x1 = Factor[ArrayReshape[Normal[vac . pgT[1, 0, {\"A\", \"F\"}, {1, 0}, G]], {4, 4}]];\n x2 = Factor[ArrayReshape[Normal[vac . pgT[2, 1/2, {\"A\", \"F\"}, {1, 0}, G]], {4, 4}]];\n x3 = Factor[ArrayReshape[Normal[vac . pgT[3, 1/2, {\"A\", \"F\"}, {1, 0}, G, True]], {4, 4}]];\n Grid[{{\"X1 = <e1 e4| t1(thF)\", MatrixForm[x1]}, {\"X2 = <e1 e4| t2(thF+h)\", MatrixForm[x2]}, {\"X3 = <e1 e4| t3^(thF+h)\", MatrixForm[x3]}}, Frame -> All]]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
